      "description": "Add a new section `## 6. Isolation-safety checklist` to the use-qwen eval runbook, carrying the three isolation-safety rules from the 2026-08-31 eval incidents as checklist items a future eval run walks instead of remembering. Documentation only: no scripts, no code, no CHANGELOG entry (docs commits need none).\n\nLocation: skills/use-qwen/references/eval-runbook.md (existing sections `## 1.` .. `## 5.` then `## Scope of approval`; insert the new section between `## 5. On pass: append to the registry` and `## Scope of approval`).\n\nReuse: the runbook's existing numbered-section structure; the compliance gate `uv run python3 skills/create-skill/scripts/validate_skill.py skills/use-qwen`; `braid --check` (repository rule after touching a skill).\n\nContract (verbatim from the design doc, C1 - insert this markdown block exactly, byte for byte):\n\n## 6. Isolation-safety checklist\n\nWalk this list every time you prepare or re-run a task. Each item is a bug that\nactually corrupted an eval run (2026-08-31): none is hypothetical, and none is\ncheap to detect after the fact.\n\n1. **Never reuse a tree whose dispatch was interrupted, and re-verify\n   pre-task state before every re-dispatch.** A killed run can leave the\n   target file(s) partly or fully fixed, and a child process (`pi`, `claude\n   --print`) can outlive the kill and keep editing. Run every dispatch under\n   a process-group timeout (GNU `timeout -k <grace> <limit> <command>`), so a\n   hung run dies whole; confirm nothing from that tree survives (`pgrep -lf\n   <tree-path>` prints nothing); then build a fresh tree for the retry and\n   leave the old one alone. Before dispatching into ANY tree, fresh or not,\n   run the task's gate against it and confirm it still fails; never score a\n   dispatch made onto a tree that passed its gate beforehand.\n2. **Before reverting a file to its pre-task state, check the file's later\n   history at line level.** First `git log --oneline --no-patch <commit>..HEAD\n   -- <path>`. Empty: no later commit touched the file, and a whole-file\n   checkout of `<commit>^ -- <path>` is safe. Non-empty: a whole-file checkout\n   is NEVER safe (it erases every later commit, on any line). For a file the\n   task ADDED, non-empty disqualifies the task outright: the pre-task state\n   is \"absent\" and no revert can both delete the file and keep the later\n   work. For a file the task CHANGED, take the line span where the task's\n   hunks sit at HEAD (from `git blame`, or the `+` side of `git diff\n   <commit>^ HEAD -- <path>`) and run `git log --oneline --no-patch\n   -L<start>,<end>:<path> <commit>..HEAD`. Do not use `-G`/`-S` pickaxe: it\n   misses in-body edits. Empty: the later changes are disjoint, so revert\n   with the task's own hunks reversed (`git diff <commit> <commit>^ --\n   <path>` as a patch), which leaves the later work in place. Non-empty:\n   write a surgical, text-level reverse patch of just the task's hunks by\n   hand (and save it before dispatch), or disqualify the task. Apply this to\n   EVERY file you intend to revert; a file left at today's state needs no\n   check.\n3. **Never run two test or build invocations concurrently against the same\n   tree.** Dispatch one engine, run its gate to completion, restore\n   pre-task state, then start the next engine. Shared build dirs and\n   file-locking test suites deadlock or cross-contaminate under concurrent\n   runs; the symptom is a hang, not an error.\n\nFor a multi-file task, items 1 and 2 apply per touched file, and the\npre-task state is the whole set reverted together.\n\nConstraints on the edit (verbatim from the design doc): keep the existing numbering (`## 1.` .. `## 5.`), add `## 6.` only, leave `## Scope of approval` last. Validate with `uv run python3 skills/create-skill/scripts/validate_skill.py skills/use-qwen` and `braid --check`.\n\nDetails:\n- The file currently has these level-2 headings in order: `## 1. Pick 6 eval tasks`, `## 2. Run each task - against the candidate, and nothing else`, `## 3. Verify against the real gate - never the model's self-report`, `## 4. Pass bar`, `## 5. On pass: append to the registry`, `## Scope of approval`. After the edit the order is the same with `## 6. Isolation-safety checklist` inserted before `## Scope of approval`.\n- Leave every existing line byte-identical; the only change is the inserted block (plus one blank line on each side so the section separates cleanly).\n- Do not touch `SKILL.md`, `approved-models.txt`, or any script.\n- The repository is public: the block contains no personal paths or names (it does not).\n\nAcceptance criteria (verbatim from the PRD, Phase 0):\n- the checklist names all three failure modes (interrupted-run contamination, whole-file-revert erasing unrelated history, concurrent same-worktree test runs) and the concrete check or rule that prevents each; a reader unfamiliar with the 2026-08-31 incident can follow it without needing this PRD as context.\n\nExit criteria (PRD): `eval-runbook.md` diff reviewed and committed.\n\nVerify:\n- `rg -n \"^## \" skills/use-qwen/references/eval-runbook.md` prints the seven headings in the order listed under Details, with `## 6. Isolation-safety checklist` sixth.\n- The inserted block matches the Contract text exactly (a test may compare the file slice between `## 6.` and `## Scope of approval` against the expected text).\n- `uv run python3 skills/create-skill/scripts/validate_skill.py skills/use-qwen` exits 0.\n- `braid --check` exits 0.\n- `uv run pytest -q` still passes (963 passed / 5 skipped at e87f23d; the count may only grow by this task's own test).\n",
